## THE DECISIONS INSIDE ROUND 2 (each Tuesday's as coordinator; each a Question at the stamp; "the stream" = `src/Datasec.Suite.UniversalPrint/Ipp/ResumableDocumentStream.cs`, 243 lines at the head)
- **D1 THE EXCEPTION OF THE CLOSE.** A read in flight when the stream is closed ends in `ObjectDisposedException`, the type a read started AFTER the close already gives (`ResumableDocumentStream.cs:82`; the existing cell `Closing_the_stream_closes_the_current_body_and_a_read_after_closing_fails` asserts it). The exception that ended the await, if there was one, is kept as its inner exception. **The caller's own cancellation still passes through unchanged:** when the caller's token is cancelled and the stream is NOT closed, the platform's cancellation exception leaves as today. When both have happened, the read ends in one of those two types and in no third (cell X10). Alternative A: the platform's cancellation exception (what the Workpath source throws: its lines 220 to 224; cost: a caller cannot tell its own cancellation from a close). Alternative B: an I/O error (the source's line 66 for a read after the close; cost: I/O errors are what the transport lane will catch in order to go on).
- **D2 THE LOOKS.** The closed flag is looked at after EVERY await of a read and before every action of section P1; "at least these points" are the table there. The look before the wait sits INSIDE `AfterFailureAsync` (first statement), so that the two lines of `:124-125` stay byte-equal (the review's mutant g15 replaces them). The look at the top of each turn goes BEFORE the lines of `:92-97` and leaves them byte-equal (mutants g13, g22).
- **D3 ONE OWNER PER ANSWER; ONE WAY TO CLOSE.** The flag and the current body are shared between a read in flight and a close that arrives on another thread (the review's own run resumed the read on a pool thread while the close ran: DERIVED from its `StreamAttack.cs:51`, `RunContinuationsAsynchronously`). So: the flag is read and written as a volatile value; the current body is taken out of its field by an atomic exchange, by whoever disposes it; **a read PUBLISHES a new body into the field and THEN looks at the flag (closed: it takes the body back out and disposes it); a close SETS the flag and THEN takes the body.** Either the close sees the body or the read sees the flag: no interleaving leaves an answer open (DERIVED; cells X01 to X05 and T01 are the proof). `DisposeAsync`, `Dispose` and `Close` all go through ONE private routine that sets the flag and takes the body; the asynchronous way disposes the body asynchronously, the other two synchronously (cell X11 tells them apart: today the review's mutant g19 survives because the asynchronous way is done twice, once by the base class: `mutants.out` line 26 and its hand-run line 40).
- **D4 THE CLOSE SENDS NO SIGNAL OF ITS OWN INTO AN AWAIT IN FLIGHT.** The close sets the flag and disposes the current body; it does not cancel the fetch function, the delay function or a body's read. So: a fetch in flight at the close runs until IT returns (its answer is then disposed and the read ends by D1); a wait in flight runs until IT returns (the stream never asks for more than 8000 ms plus 299: `:154`, `:227`); a body's read that neither ends when its body is disposed nor honours the token it was handed NEVER ends (the review measured this row at the head and did not make it a finding: its log rows 126 and 127). **Each is a NAMED limit in the XML comment and a cell that states it (X01 rows 7 and 8, X03, X05).** Alternative C: a private cancellation source, cancelled by the close and linked to the caller's token for the span of one read, so that a fetch, a wait or a body's read that honours its token ends at once (costs: the token the three functions receive is no longer the caller's own, so a cancellation exception no longer carries the caller's token; one allocation per read; a second shared object with its own disposal races, in a round that has no successor).
- **D5 THE REFUSAL OF A SECOND READ.** A read (either `ReadAsync` overload, and through them `CopyToAsync` and the framework's other reading helpers) started while another is in flight is refused with `InvalidOperationException` BEFORE it touches any state: one flag, taken by an atomic compare-and-set after the disposed look, the token look and the empty-buffer return (`:82-87`, which stay byte-equal: mutant g27), released in a `finally` by the read that took it and by no other. Because the take is atomic the refusal also holds when the two reads start on two threads at the same instant. Alternative D: `NotSupportedException`.
- **D6 THE TWO INVARIANTS, WHATEVER THE CALLER DOES.** (a) After a body's read returns a count, the stream compares it with what it asked for AND with what remains to the total at that moment: `read > wanted || read > _totalBytes - _offset` is a `DocumentFetchException` that names the offset, the body is closed, the offset does not move, nothing is retried (a body that reports more than it was asked for is not a transient failure). (b) An answer whose range start is not the offset asked for is refused before its body is read: that is the head's own check (`:194`, the first seat's mutant m6 kills it) and you change no byte of it. **The first half of (a) has its own cell (R05). The second half can only fire when two reads run at once, which D5 refuses: it has NO cell that can fail while the refusal stands, and it is proved by a PAIR of mutants in Step 6(a) (the refusal removed: R01's count assertion stays green; the refusal and the comparison removed: it is red). Question Q5 asks whether a statement with that kind of proof stays; the default keeps it.**
- **D7 THE RESERVED GROUP DELIMITERS ARE TAKEN.** `IppTag.IsKnownGroup` (`IppTag.cs:46-47`, used by the reader at `IppReader.cs:57` and by the writer at `IppWriter.cs:30`) is true for 0x01, 0x02, 0x04, 0x05 AND for 0x06 to 0x0f; 0x00 and 0x03 stay as they are (0x00 refused by reader and writer; 0x03 is the end tag). A group with such a tag is read with its attributes and carries its tag byte; the writer writes it back; the round trip holds. Section THE DELIMITERS quotes the public standard and the public reference reader. Alternative E: strict, as the head is (cost: ONE such delimiter in an answer ends every document of the stream with no retry: RELAYED, the review's row S5).
- **D8 THE COST OF A HOSTILE PACKET IS NAMED, NOT BOUNDED, IN THIS ROUND** (section THE 80 MIB). Alternative F: a cap on the number of groups. Alternative G: a constructor parameter of the stream for the bound of the dropped packet, default 1 MiB.
- **D9 THE RETRY RULE STAYS AS CODED AND ITS LIMIT IS NAMED** (section THE RETRY BOUND). Alternative H: a ceiling on fetches per document as a named constant with a constructor parameter.
- **D10 THE ASYNCHRONOUS CELLS HAVE A HANG GUARD AND NO SLEEP.** A cell that awaits a read which a wrong fix would leave pending for ever carries a bound that only a FAILING run reaches (the test attribute's own timeout if this runner honours it, else `WaitAsync` with 30 s around the awaited task: UNMEASURED which; measure with one scratch cell and say). It is not a sleep: a green run never waits for it. No cell waits for a time to pass, reads a clock or uses `Task.Delay`: where the review's program sleeps 20 ms to let a read reach the body (`StreamAttack.cs:266`), your fake completes a task when the read has reached it and the cell awaits that task.
- **D11 THE BRANCH MOVES BY ONE COMMIT, FAST-FORWARD;** at most ONE more commit of yours if the hosted run is red at a cause in your diff; then STOP.

