## P1: THE CLOSE (finding 1; closed list, by PROPERTY first)
**The property.** After your commit: **once the stream is closed (by `DisposeAsync`, `Dispose` or `Close`, from any thread, once or many times, before, between or during reads), NO fetch starts, NO wait begins, NO read of any body is started, NO read returns a count to a caller, and EVERY answer the stream holds at the close or receives after it is disposed. A read in flight at the close ends in `ObjectDisposedException` (D1) as soon as the await it is in returns.** The caller's own cancellation is not changed by any of this.
**One clause of Tuesday's ruling is reached in its sense and not in its letter, and you say so in STATUS:** "no byte is written into any caller's buffer". The stream starts no body read after the close and reports no count; but a body's read that was ALREADY in flight at the close was handed the caller's buffer before the close, and if that body completes the read with bytes while it is being disposed, those bytes are in the buffer although the read ends in the exception and reports none. Closing that in the letter needs a buffer of the stream's own and a copy on every read. Default (Question Q2): NAMED limit in the XML comment, cell X08. If Stamp S4 rules the copy, S4 wins.
**The review's reproduction (RELAYED; its program `StreamAttack.cs`, a copy readable by you at `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/TUESDAY/2_Project_Files/fleet/state/s301/d1802/gate/probes/Fuzz/StreamAttack.cs`, 426 lines; its result `gate/logs/fuzz-stream.log` beside it, 140 lines; MEASURED [F4] that both copies are byte-equal to the review's files).** The fake body is its class `Body` (`StreamAttack.cs:6-73`): a read that would start at a chosen body position stays pending (`:49-56`) until the test releases it (`:60`) or the body is disposed, and its field `OnDispose` (`:14`) says how a pending read ends at the disposal (`:62-72`): 0 an I/O error, 1 it returns 0, 2 `ObjectDisposedException`, 3 nothing (it stays pending). The row (`:260-277`): a document of 10 bytes; the first read takes 4; a second read is held in the body at document byte 4; the stream is closed; the pending read is given 1500 ms; then one more read is tried. **What it printed at the head (rows 120 to 127 of its log; MEASURED [F4] by reading the copy):**
```
row | way of closing | the body ends the held read with | the held read | fetches after the close | waits after the close | bodies left open | a read after the close
1 | DisposeAsync | an I/O error | RETURNS 4 bytes | 1 | 1 | 0 of 2 | ObjectDisposedException
2 | Dispose | an I/O error | RETURNS 4 bytes | 1 | 1 | 1 of 2 | ObjectDisposedException
3 | DisposeAsync | 0 bytes | RETURNS 4 bytes | 1 | 1 | 1 of 2 | ObjectDisposedException
4 | Dispose | 0 bytes | RETURNS 4 bytes | 1 | 1 | 1 of 2 | ObjectDisposedException
5 | DisposeAsync | ObjectDisposedException | ends with ObjectDisposedException | 0 | 0 | 0 of 1 | ObjectDisposedException
6 | Dispose | ObjectDisposedException | ends with ObjectDisposedException | 0 | 0 | 0 of 1 | ObjectDisposedException
7 | DisposeAsync | nothing | NEVER ends | 0 | 0 | 0 of 1 | ObjectDisposedException
8 | Dispose | nothing | NEVER ends | 0 | 0 | 0 of 1 | ObjectDisposedException
```
**After your commit (DERIVED; UNMEASURED until your cells run): rows 1 to 6 read "ends with ObjectDisposedException | 0 | 0 | 0 of 1"; rows 7 and 8 are unchanged (D4's named limit).** Why the head fails rows 1 to 4 (DERIVED from `:110-125`): the disposed body ends the read with an I/O error or with 0 bytes; both are a failed read; the loop closes the body, asks for the wait, comes round, looks at the token only, opens again, and the new body's first read gives 4 bytes to the read that was abandoned.
**Table LOOKS: at least these points (D2). At each, a closed stream ends the read in D1's exception and does nothing else; any other await or action of a read that you add or find: prove it meets the property or STOP.**
