**Any other thing you meet while building: add a row, say closed, limit or out, and name it FIRST in FOR TUESDAY.**
**THREAD-SAFETY, IN PLAIN WORDS (the comment says exactly this much and no more): "One read at a time: a second read while one is in flight is refused. The stream may be closed from any thread at any time. Nothing else about it is safe to use from two threads at once."**

## THE DELIMITERS (finding 3; D7; a Question, Stamp S4 rules)
**The public standard** (MEASURED [F5]: RFC 8010, a copy fetched by GET from a public repository, 2859 lines, git blob `51127c81b06d988b6a0f8ca7af37392665b2350b`, the same blob in nine unrelated public repositories; the drafter's copy is `.../fleet/state/s301/d1802/public/rfc8010.txt`, readable by you as a claim). Its grammar, line 824: "begin-attribute-group-tag = %x00-02 / %x04-0f ; see Section 3.5.1". Line 860: "future-group-tags          = %x06-0f          ; future extensions". Its table 2 (lines 1052 to 1057) names 0x00 "Reserved" and 0x01 to 0x05. Lines 1088 to 1091: "A Printer MUST treat a "delimiter-tag" (values from 0x00 through 0x0f) differently from a "value-tag" (values from 0x10 through 0xff) so that the Printer knows there is an entire attribute group as opposed to a single value." **So the standard's text reads the tolerant way for 0x06 to 0x0f: they are attribute groups of later documents. It does not say what a reader does with 0x00.**
**The public register and the public reference reader** (RELAYED from the verdict's finding 3, re-read by the drafter in the first seat's copies `<project>/.tools/b1788/scratch/public/libcups_ipp.h` and `libcups_ipp.c`, MEASURED [F5]): the header's lines 456 to 460 list five registered groups after the unsupported group (subscription, event notification, resource, document, system: 0x06 to 0x0a by their place in the list); the reader's lines 5479 to 5484 refuse the zero tag (and an operation group that is not the first); its lines 5485 to 5508 take EVERY other byte below 0x10 as a group tag. **You copy no line of either file.**
**The cost of each reading.** Tolerant (the default): one changed test in `IppTag.IsKnownGroup` (MEASURED [F2]: the reader and the writer call it at one place each); no cost in memory or time (the same bounds apply: RELAYED, the review's reasoning, not run); the only loss is that a packet holding a delimiter nobody registered is no longer refused. Strict (the head): one such delimiter in the answers to a fetch ends every document, not retried (RELAYED: the review's row S5: the format exception, 0 bytes, 0 waits, the body disposed). **Whether the print service ever sends such a group is UNMEASURED, and you say so in STATUS.**
| Cell | What it does | Required | Its red |
|---|---|---|---|
| G01 | each tag 0x06 to 0x0f as the FIRST group and as a SECOND group after an operation group, each with one attribute in it (20 rows) | read without an exception; the group carries its tag and its attribute; **writing what was read gives the input byte for byte** | RED on the head: all 20 (the rows of the existing theory `IppReaderTests.cs:218`, which now keeps 0x00 only) |
| G02 | the writer: a group tagged 0x06 and one tagged 0x0f are written; 0x00, 0x03 and 0x21 are still refused; the reader still refuses 0x00 as first and as second group | as said | RED on the head for 0x06 and 0x0f |
| G03 | through the stream: an answer whose dropped packet holds a group 0x09 (the review's row: `StreamAttack.cs:200` with its tag 0x06) | the document bytes are yielded; `LastPartHeader` holds that group with its tag | RED on the head |
Your mutant mD (the test of `IsKnownGroup` back to the four): G01 RED. The message texts of `IppReader.cs:59` and `IppWriter.cs:32` say "is no known group": after D7 they are still true of 0x00, 0x03 and 0x21; change neither unless a cell shows one untrue.

## THE 80 MIB (finding 4; D8; a Question, Stamp S4 rules)
**The review's measured input** (RELAYED; `gate/logs/fuzz-worst.log`, 23 lines, MEASURED [F4] by reading the copy): 1 MiB of alternating group delimiters and then the end tag, 1,048,575 bytes: accepted, 103 ms, "allocated 80.0 MiB in all"; the endless variant is refused at the bound after the same 80.0 MiB. Other shapes: 36.0 MiB (attributes of six bytes), 21.6 MiB (members of one collection), 18.9 MiB (collections 32 deep), 12.0 MiB (further values), 1.0 MiB (values of 32,767 bytes). Under a caller's own bound of 64 MiB: 5120 MiB and 5612 ms. No single read above 32,767 bytes was ever asked of the source.
**What a legitimate answer needs: the review's files do NOT show it.** No captured answer exists (RELAYED: the verdict's NOT TESTED list). The review's sentence that the dropped packet of a fetched part "is tiny" is its judgement, not a measurement; an answer to Get-Jobs holds one group per job, and how many jobs one answer may hold is UNMEASURED. **So, by Tuesday's own rule for this point (the bound only if the files show what a legitimate answer needs, else the named limit), the default is the NAMED LIMIT:** one sentence in `IppReader`'s comment: within its bound the reader may allocate up to about eighty times the bytes it reads, and a caller that reads from an untrusted peer passes a small bound. No code, no cell (the review's `worst` rows are re-run by the review). A cap on the number of groups (alternative F) removes the 80.0 MiB shape and leaves the 36.0 MiB one; a bound parameter on the stream (alternative G) changes the text of the review's mutants g30 and g31 (`:204`) and adds a public parameter. Both go to FOR RECORDS as the transport lane's choice.

## THE RETRY BOUND (records point 6b; D9; a Question, Stamp S4 rules)
**What the Workpath source does** (MEASURED [F6] by the drafter's reading of the stream file, 260 lines, sha256 as the first kit's FACT 3): its lines 82 to 86 set the attempt count to 0 whenever the offset differs from the last one looked at; its lines 194 to 203 refuse only when the count at one offset has reached the limit; nothing counts fetches per document. **The port does the same** (`:97-101`, `:220`): an answer that gives one byte and then fails makes progress every time, so the limit of three never applies (RELAYED: the review's row, 50 fetches and 49 waits of 1007 ms for 50 bytes).
**The two ways, and what each costs.** Keep as coded and name it (the default): one sentence in the stream's comment and cell N01; the caller already has the means to stop a slow drip (its fetch function may throw, and a fetch that throws is not retried: the existing cell `A_fetch_that_throws_is_not_retried`; or it cancels its token); no number is invented. A ceiling on fetches per document (alternative H): a new public constructor parameter, a new exception path, three cells, and a NUMBER that nobody has measured (how many resumes a document of 1 GB over a poor link legitimately needs is UNMEASURED; the verdict calls a ceiling "a product choice").

