## HEAVY (four seats build at once on ONE machine)
A heavy job = `dotnet restore`, `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, and each run of the review's generator in Step 6(k). **ONE heavy job at a time on the whole project, never in the background.** Before each: (1) **the load gate, in ONE stated reading:** two `LC_ALL=C uptime` reads at least 120 s apart, both with load1 under 24, the later one no older than 900 s when the job starts (not independent: say so; this is the reading the first seat used: RELAYED). **Load reads come BEFORE the lock, never inside it.** (2) **the wave's lock, the ONE folder all seats of this wave share: `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/!CODING/Datasec/OXPd_2/.tools/wave1-heavy.lock.d`.** Take it with `mkdir` of EXACTLY that path (rc 0 means you hold it), write `owner.txt` inside it (your brief number, your pane id, the command, `date`), run the ONE command, then release it by MOVING the folder to `<lane>/_quarantine/locks/<a name made from date>` (`mv`, never `rm`, never `rmdir`). **If you run heavy jobs through a runner program, it makes the lock folder exactly at that path and NOWHERE else under `<project>/.tools`. You never take, move or touch another seat's lock.** The lock wraps the heavy command only: never a wait, a scan or a git call. `mkdir` fails: wait 30 s and try again, for at most 30 minutes; then `BLOCKED: lock held <the owner.txt line>`. An `owner.txt` older than 30 minutes is NOT yours to break: `STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY stale lock <its line>`. After each heavy command and before the release: `pgrep -fl 'b1802'`; a build server or test host of yours still alive is stopped by its pid and named in STATUS. **A test run that does not end (a held read that a wrong fix leaves pending, with no guard) holds the lock against three other seats: that is why D10's guard is not optional; a run older than 10 minutes is stopped by its pid, the lock released, and the cell that hung named.** A wait is not a finding; at the hard stop `BLOCKED: load <n>` or `BLOCKED: lock`.

